steps.body:
A booking request says which room, when it starts and ends, and how many people are coming, with an optional note. Here is everything its schema checks, and why:

- **Required fields and types**: `roomId` and `attendees` are whole numbers, `startsAt` and `endsAt` are times, and `note` is text or absent.
- **Formats**: times are written in **RFC 3339**, the standard text form of a date and time that JSON APIs use, like `2026-10-05T14:00:00Z` or `2026-10-05T16:00:00+02:00`. A time without an offset from UTC is refused, since `14:00` alone means a different moment in every time zone.
- **Ranges and lengths**: `roomId` is positive, `attendees` is 1 to 30, the most any room holds, and `note` is at most 500 characters. Limits like these protect the server as much as the data. A note with no limit invites someone to store a megabyte of text in every booking.
- **No unknown fields.** A client that sends `notes` instead of `note` learns about its typo at once, instead of wondering why the note never saved. A client that tries to set `status` or `userId` itself is told no, instead of having its field quietly kept, or quietly dropped.
- **Rules across fields**: `endsAt` must be after `startsAt`. That needs nothing but the request itself, so the schema can check it.

## Normalize on the way in

Parsing can also tidy what it accepts, so the rest of the code only ever sees one form of each value. This is called **normalizing**. Roombook trims spaces from the ends of the note, and converts both times to UTC, so `16:00+02:00` and `14:00Z` become the same moment. From the authentication lesson on, emails are lowercased the same way.

In Spring, the schema is the request record itself, with Bean Validation annotations on its fields, in `bookings/CreateBookingRequest.java`:

```java
@EndsAfterStart
public record CreateBookingRequest(
        @NotNull @Positive Long roomId,
        @NotNull Instant startsAt,
        @NotNull Instant endsAt,
        @NotNull @Min(1) @Max(30) Integer attendees,
        @Size(max = 500) String note) {

    public CreateBookingRequest {
        note = note == null ? null : note.strip();
    }
}
```

- `@NotNull` makes a field required. The fields are boxed types like `Long`, not `long`, so a missing field is `null` and gets caught, instead of silently becoming `0`.
- `@Positive`, `@Min`, `@Max`, and `@Size` set the ranges and lengths.
- Jackson reads `Instant` from RFC 3339 text, offset included, so `16:00+02:00` arrives as `14:00Z`, and text without an offset is refused.
- The compact constructor, the block with no parameter list, trims the note as the record is built.

`@Valid` on the controller's parameter, `@Valid @RequestBody CreateBookingRequest body`, tells Spring to run the checks before your method does. Two settings in `application.yaml` make Jackson stricter than Spring Boot's defaults:

```yaml
spring:
  jackson:
    deserialization:
      fail-on-unknown-properties: true
    mapper:
      allow-coercion-of-scalars: false
```

The first refuses unknown fields, and the second refuses `"1"` where a number belongs. The rule across fields is a constraint of your own, an annotation on the whole record, with a validator that reports on `endsAt`:

```java
@Target(ElementType.TYPE)
@Retention(RetentionPolicy.RUNTIME)
@Constraint(validatedBy = EndsAfterStart.Validator.class)
public @interface EndsAfterStart {
    String message() default "must be after startsAt";
    Class<?>[] groups() default {};
    Class<? extends Payload>[] payload() default {};

    class Validator implements ConstraintValidator<EndsAfterStart, CreateBookingRequest> {
        @Override
        public boolean isValid(CreateBookingRequest b, ConstraintValidatorContext context) {
            if (b.startsAt() == null || b.endsAt() == null || b.endsAt().isAfter(b.startsAt())) {
                return true;
            }
            context.disableDefaultConstraintViolation();
            context.buildConstraintViolationWithTemplate(context.getDefaultConstraintMessageTemplate())
                    .addPropertyNode("endsAt")
                    .addConstraintViolation();
            return false;
        }
    }
}
```

Jackson's checks and Bean Validation's run at different moments, and the example shows what that means. Jackson converts the JSON first, and stops at the first value it can't convert. Only a body that converts cleanly reaches Bean Validation, which reports every broken rule at once.

## One schema per operation

Creating, replacing, and changing part of a booking need different schemas, even though they describe the same thing. A create requires `roomId`. A PATCH, the method from the routes lesson for changing part of a resource, can't move a booking to another room, so it has no `roomId` at all, and every field it does have is optional. Reusing the create schema for PATCH would demand fields the client didn't mean to change, and would let it send ones it mustn't. A PATCH also has to tell a field that's missing, which means leave it alone, from one sent as `null`, which means clear it, as the routes lesson explained.

Here is the PATCH record, in `bookings/BookingPatch.java`, with every field optional. It holds only what the request data lesson's PATCH handler changes, the attendees and the note, with the same limits as the create schema. A plain record still can't tell a missing `note` from `"note": null`, since both arrive as `null`, so the handler keeps that lesson's `JsonNode` and its `has("note")` check for that one question:

```java
public record BookingPatch(
        @Min(1) @Max(30) Integer attendees,
        @Size(max = 500) String note) {}
```

Path parameters, query strings, and headers arrive as text too, and they need the same care, with two differences. Everything in them is a string, so parsing means converting text into numbers, lists, and true or false. And most of them are optional, so each needs a **default**, the value you use when the client sends none.

## Query strings

`GET /v1/rooms` takes five query parameters, and each has a rule:

| Parameter | Parsed as | Default | Limits |
|---|---|---|---|
| `building` | a building's slug, like `main-library` | any building | lowercase letters, digits, dashes |
| `minCapacity` | a whole number | 1 | 1 to 30 |
| `amenity` | a list, from repeated keys: `amenity=whiteboard&amenity=screen` | none | |
| `limit` | a whole number | 20 | 1 to 100 |
| `cursor` | the opaque cursor from the last page | the first page | must be one the API issued |

Roombook refuses query parameters it doesn't know, the same as unknown body fields. A typo like `minCapcity=6` then gets a 422 naming it, instead of a full, unfiltered list that looks right.

## Bounds protect the server

A `limit` with no maximum lets one request ask for every row in the table, and a few of those at once can slow the server for everyone. So every value that controls how much work the server does gets an upper bound: page sizes, date ranges, and the length of any list. Roombook refuses `limit=500` instead of quietly lowering it to 100, so the client learns the rule instead of wondering why it got fewer rooms than it asked for.

## Cursors and ids are input too

The `nextCursor` the API hands out comes back in the next request. Clients aren't supposed to look inside one, but nothing stops them from changing it, so decode it as carefully as any other input. Roombook's cursor is base64url text holding a little JSON, so parsing it means decoding, reading the JSON, and checking for a positive id, and any failure is a 422 on `cursor`, never a crash.

An id in the path is different. A path that names nothing is a 404, as the routes lesson said, so `/v1/bookings/abc` gets one. Check the range too. `99999999999999999999` is digits, but it doesn't fit in the 64-bit integer the database uses for ids, so it can't name a booking either, and it's a 404 as well.

## Headers

Headers get the same treatment. The request id middleware from the middleware lesson already checks `X-Request-Id` before trusting it, and body parsing checks `Content-Type` and answers 415. The data integrity and reliability lessons parse `If-Match` and `Idempotency-Key` the same way: a format check, and a clear error when it fails.

Spring binds query parameters to a record when the controller takes one, and validates it like a body when the parameter has `@Valid`, in `rooms/ListRoomsQuery.java`:

```java
public record ListRoomsQuery(
        @Pattern(regexp = "[a-z0-9-]{1,40}") String building,
        @Min(1) @Max(30) Integer minCapacity,
        List<String> amenity,
        @Min(1) @Max(100) Integer limit,
        String cursor) {

    public ListRoomsQuery {
        minCapacity = minCapacity == null ? 1 : minCapacity;
        amenity = amenity == null ? List.of() : amenity;
        limit = limit == null ? 20 : limit;
    }
}
```

```java
@GetMapping
RoomPage list(@Valid ListRoomsQuery query) {
```

The boxed `Integer` fields are `null` when the parameter is missing, and the compact constructor fills in the defaults before validation runs, so `@Max(100)` checks the final `limit`. `List<String> amenity` collects repeated keys. A value that doesn't convert, like `minCapacity=lots`, is a binding error. Spring reports it as a field error, but can't build the record without it, so constraint checks on the other fields don't run, and it comes back alone. The cursor is decoded in the controller, which throws a `validation-failed` error on `cursor` when it fails, as part 6 shows.

For the booking id in the path, the mapping is `@GetMapping("/{bookingId:\\d{1,18}}")`. The pattern accepts 1 to 18 digits, which always fit in a `long`, so `abc` and the too-big id match no route at all, and get 404.

Not every check can happen at the edge. The schema knows what a booking request looks like, but not whether the Lovelace room holds five people, whether Ada already has three bookings, or whether someone booked Turing for 14:00 a second ago. Those need data, and the last one needs the database itself. So Roombook checks requests at three checkpoints:

```diagram validation-and-errors-checkpoints
Each checkpoint catches what the one before it can't, and answers with its own codes.
```

1. **The edge** checks **shape**: types, formats, ranges, required and unknown fields, and rules within the request itself. It needs nothing but the request, so it runs first and costs almost nothing. A body that can't be read at all, like broken JSON, is a **400 Bad Request**. A body in the wrong format, like text sent as `text/plain`, is a **415**. JSON that's readable but the wrong shape is a **422 Unprocessable Content**.
2. **The service**, the code that holds Roombook's business rules, which the next lesson gives its own layer, checks the rules that need data or the clock. A booking starts and ends on the hour or half past, lasts 30 minutes to 3 hours, starts in the future and at most 14 days ahead, and fits the room's capacity. And a member holds at most 3 upcoming bookings.
3. **The database** has the last word on anything two requests could race on: two bookings for the same room at the same time, or two accounts with the same email. Only the database sees every write as it happens. A check in your code reads the data and then writes, and between the two, another request can slip in. The data integrity lesson builds that rule into the database, and until then, the in-memory store plays its part.

## 400, 409, or 422

| Code | Means | Roombook examples |
|---|---|---|
| 400 Bad Request | The request can't be read at all | Broken JSON, data after the JSON value |
| 415 Unsupported Media Type | The body isn't in a format the API takes | `Content-Type: text/plain` |
| 422 Unprocessable Content | Readable, but the content breaks a rule | `attendees` of 0, an unknown field, too many people for the room |
| 409 Conflict | Fine on its own, but it clashes with data the server has now | The room is already booked, three bookings are already held |

The line between 422 and 409 takes a moment to see. Ask: would the same request succeed later, if something else changed? An overlap clears when the other booking is cancelled, and the booking limit clears when one of Ada's bookings ends. Those are conflicts, 409. Five people in a room for four is wrong no matter what anyone else does, so the request itself has to change. That's 422.

## Rules report fields too

The service's rules are about fields too, so it reports them the way the schema does: a field name and a message for each, and all of them at once. A client then handles a broken rule exactly like a broken format, with the message next to the right input.

Here are the rules in `BookingService.create`, in `bookings/BookingService.java`:

```java
    public Booking create(NewBooking in) {
        Room room = rooms.findById(in.roomId()).orElseThrow(() ->
                new UnprocessableException("room-not-found", "That room doesn't exist.", null));

        Instant now = clock.instant();
        Duration length = Duration.between(in.startsAt(), in.endsAt());
        List<FieldViolation> errors = new ArrayList<>();
        if (!onHalfHour(in.startsAt())) errors.add(new FieldViolation("startsAt", "must be on the hour or half past"));
        if (!onHalfHour(in.endsAt())) errors.add(new FieldViolation("endsAt", "must be on the hour or half past"));
        if (length.toMinutes() < 30 || length.toMinutes() > 180) errors.add(new FieldViolation("endsAt", "a booking lasts 30 minutes to 3 hours"));
        if (!in.startsAt().isAfter(now)) errors.add(new FieldViolation("startsAt", "must be in the future"));
        if (in.startsAt().isAfter(now.plus(Duration.ofDays(14)))) errors.add(new FieldViolation("startsAt", "must be at most 14 days ahead"));
        if (in.attendees() > room.capacity()) errors.add(new FieldViolation("attendees", room.name() + " holds at most " + room.capacity() + " people"));
        if (!errors.isEmpty()) throw UnprocessableException.invalidFields(errors);

        if (bookings.countUpcoming(in.userId(), now) >= 3) {
            throw new ConflictException("booking-limit", "You already have 3 upcoming bookings.", null);
        }
        return bookings.insert(in);
    }
```

`UnprocessableException`, `ConflictException`, and `FieldViolation` are Roombook's own, from `common/`, which part 6 introduces. A room id that names no room is its own problem, `room-not-found`. It's a 422, not a 404: the URL, `/v1/bookings`, exists, and it's the request's content that's wrong. It's checked first and alone, since without the room there's no capacity to check against. The other rules all add to `errors`, and `UnprocessableException.invalidFields` throws them together, as one `validation-failed` error. The limit check comes only after every rule passes, since it costs a lookup in the store.

Clients have to understand your errors as well as your successes. A web app wants to show each message beside the right form field, a script wants to know whether trying again could help, and a developer wants enough to fix their request. If each endpoint invents its own error shape, every client writes special cases for each one. Roombook uses one shape for every error, the **Problem Details** format from RFC 9457, which the lesson on what an API is introduced.

## The standard members

A problem is a JSON object sent with the media type `application/problem+json`, so a client can tell an error body from a normal one by its `Content-Type` alone. RFC 9457 defines five members, all optional:

| Member | Holds | Example |
|---|---|---|
| `type` | A URI that names the kind of problem | `https://roombook.example/problems/booking-overlap` |
| `title` | A short summary of that kind, the same every time | `Room already booked` |
| `status` | The HTTP status code, repeated in the body | `409` |
| `detail` | What went wrong this time, for a person to read | `The room is already booked then.` |
| `instance` | Which occurrence it was. Roombook uses the request's path | `/v1/bookings` |

`type` is the member clients act on. Code compares the type, never the words in `title` or `detail`, which you're free to reword. So a type URI, once published, keeps its meaning for good. It doesn't have to load anything, but a page there that explains the problem is good manners. When a problem has no `type`, the RFC says to read it as `about:blank`, which means nothing beyond the status code. Roombook gives every problem a type of its own instead, so a client can always switch on `type` and never needs a fallback.

## Roombook's problem types

| `type`, after `https://roombook.example/problems/` | Status | `title` |
|---|---|---|
| `invalid-json` | 400 | Body is not valid JSON |
| `not-found` | 404 | Resource not found |
| `method-not-allowed` | 405 | Method not allowed |
| `body-too-large` | 413 | Request body too large |
| `unsupported-media-type` | 415 | Unsupported media type |
| `validation-failed` | 422 | Validation failed |
| `room-not-found` | 422 | Room not found |
| `booking-overlap` | 409 | Room already booked |
| `booking-limit` | 409 | Too many upcoming bookings |
| `internal-error` | 500 | Internal server error |

The database lessons add `room-name-taken`, `email-taken`, and `stale-booking`, and the authentication lesson adds its own problems, always under the same address.

## Two members of Roombook's own

The RFC lets you add members of your own, called **extension members**, and Roombook adds two:

- `requestId`, the id from the middleware lesson, on every problem, so a user can quote it and you can find the request's log lines.
- `errors`, on `validation-failed`, a list with one entry per broken field: `field`, the field's path, and `message`. A path names a field the way you'd reach it in the JSON, like `endsAt`, or `items.0.name` for a field inside a list.

Always report every field error at once. A form with three mistakes should get one answer with three errors, not three round trips that each reveal one more.

Spring has Problem Details built in: `ProblemDetail` is a class with the five standard members, and `setProperty` adds extension members. Spring's own error handling already answers with `ProblemDetail`, as the 404 in the middleware lesson showed, with no `type` at all, which the RFC reads as `about:blank`. Roombook's advice fills in the rest, in `common/ApiExceptionHandler.java`:

```java
    private static final String TYPES = "https://roombook.example/problems/";
    private static final Map<Integer, String> BY_STATUS = Map.of(
            400, "invalid-json", 404, "not-found", 405, "method-not-allowed",
            413, "body-too-large", 415, "unsupported-media-type", 500, "internal-error");
    private static final Map<String, String> TITLES = Map.ofEntries(
            Map.entry("invalid-json", "Body is not valid JSON"),
            Map.entry("not-found", "Resource not found"),
            Map.entry("method-not-allowed", "Method not allowed"),
            Map.entry("body-too-large", "Request body too large"),
            Map.entry("unsupported-media-type", "Unsupported media type"),
            Map.entry("validation-failed", "Validation failed"),
            Map.entry("room-not-found", "Room not found"),
            Map.entry("booking-overlap", "Room already booked"),
            Map.entry("booking-limit", "Too many upcoming bookings"),
            Map.entry("internal-error", "Internal server error"));
```

```java
    private static ProblemDetail typed(HttpStatus status, String type, String detail) {
        ProblemDetail problem = ProblemDetail.forStatusAndDetail(status, detail);
        problem.setType(URI.create(TYPES + type));
        problem.setTitle(TITLES.getOrDefault(type, type));
        return problem;
    }

    private static ProblemDetail complete(ProblemDetail problem) {
        String standard = BY_STATUS.get(problem.getStatus());
        if (standard != null && problem.getType() == null) {
            problem.setType(URI.create(TYPES + standard));
            problem.setTitle(TITLES.get(standard));
        }
        problem.setInstance(URI.create(ServletUriComponentsBuilder.fromCurrentRequestUri()
                .replaceQuery(null).build().getPath()));
        problem.setProperty("requestId", MDC.get("requestId"));
        return problem;
    }
}
```

`TITLES` holds each problem type's title, in one place, so every problem of a type reads the same, and `BY_STATUS` names the type for the plain HTTP errors. `typed()` builds a problem from a status, a slug, and a detail. `complete()` runs on every problem the app sends. When Spring left the type empty, it fills in Roombook's type and title for the status. Then it adds `instance`, the path without its query string, and `requestId` from the MDC.

Jackson writes `ProblemDetail`'s members in its own order, with `detail` first, which you'll see in the examples. The order of members never matters in JSON, so clients don't notice.

Every check in this lesson ends in an error, and they come from all over: the framework when the JSON is broken, the schema library when a field is wrong, the service when a rule fails, the store when a booking overlaps, and anywhere at all when there's a bug. Turning each into a response on the spot would scatter status codes and JSON through every handler, each a little different. Instead, code reports what went wrong with an error, and one central handler, the error handling from the middleware lesson grown up, turns every error into a problem.

## Domain errors

The service and the store don't build responses. They report which kind of failure happened, and which problem it was, by its slug, and leave the rest to the central handler. Roombook has a few broad kinds:

- **Not found**: the thing asked for doesn't exist, a 404 `not-found`.
- **Unprocessable**: the request is readable but can't be done as written, a 422. It carries its slug, like `room-not-found` or `validation-failed`, and for `validation-failed`, the list of field errors.
- **Conflict**: the request clashes with current data, a 409. It carries its slug, like `booking-overlap` or `booking-limit`.

The database lessons raise these same kinds from the database: `room-name-taken` and `email-taken` are conflicts, so the central handler needs no change for them. Only `stale-booking`, a 412 for an out-of-date `If-Match`, is a new kind, in the data integrity lesson.

Here they are, in `common/`. Each is an unchecked exception, so services can throw them without declaring them, and none has any Spring in it:

```java
public class UnprocessableException extends RuntimeException {
    private final String type;
    private final List<FieldViolation> violations;

    public UnprocessableException(String type, String detail, Throwable cause) {
        this(type, detail, cause, List.of());
    }

    private UnprocessableException(String type, String detail, Throwable cause, List<FieldViolation> violations) {
        super(detail, cause);
        this.type = type;
        this.violations = List.copyOf(violations);
    }

    public static UnprocessableException invalidFields(List<FieldViolation> violations) {
        String detail = violations.size() == 1 ? "1 field is invalid." : violations.size() + " fields are invalid.";
        return new UnprocessableException("validation-failed", detail, null, violations);
    }

    public String type() {
        return type;
    }

    public List<FieldViolation> violations() {
        return violations;
    }
}
```

`ConflictException` has the same shape without the field list, and `NotFoundException` takes just a message. Each takes the problem type's slug, a detail for people to read, and the exception that caused it, if any: `new ConflictException("booking-overlap", "The room is already booked then.", null)`. `invalidFields` builds the one `UnprocessableException` that carries field errors.

## The mapping

`ApiExceptionHandler`, the controller advice from the middleware lesson, has an `@ExceptionHandler` for each of Roombook's exceptions, and still has the catch-all for everything else:

```java
    @ExceptionHandler(UnprocessableException.class)
    ProblemDetail unprocessable(UnprocessableException ex) {
        ProblemDetail problem = typed(HttpStatus.UNPROCESSABLE_CONTENT, ex.type(), ex.getMessage());
        if (!ex.violations().isEmpty()) {
            problem.setProperty("errors", ex.violations());
        }
        return complete(problem);
    }

    @ExceptionHandler(NotFoundException.class)
    ProblemDetail notFound(NotFoundException ex) {
        return complete(ProblemDetail.forStatusAndDetail(HttpStatus.NOT_FOUND, ex.getMessage()));
    }

    @ExceptionHandler(ConflictException.class)
    ProblemDetail conflict(ConflictException ex) {
        return complete(typed(HttpStatus.CONFLICT, ex.type(), ex.getMessage()));
    }

    @ExceptionHandler(Exception.class)
    ProblemDetail unexpected(Exception ex) {
        log.error("unhandled error", ex);
        return complete(ProblemDetail.forStatusAndDetail(HttpStatus.INTERNAL_SERVER_ERROR,
                "Something went wrong on our side. Quote the request id if you report it."));
    }
```

It also overrides the base class's answers to some of Spring's own exceptions:

```java
    protected ResponseEntity<Object> handleMethodArgumentNotValid(MethodArgumentNotValidException ex,
            HttpHeaders headers, HttpStatusCode status, WebRequest request) {
        List<FieldViolation> violations = ex.getBindingResult().getFieldErrors().stream()
                .map(e -> new FieldViolation(e.getField(),
                        e.isBindingFailure() ? "has the wrong type" : e.getDefaultMessage()))
                .toList();
        return ResponseEntity.unprocessableContent().body(unprocessable(UnprocessableException.invalidFields(violations)));
    }

    @Override
    protected ResponseEntity<Object> handleHttpMessageNotReadable(HttpMessageNotReadableException ex,
            HttpHeaders headers, HttpStatusCode status, WebRequest request) {
        if (ex.getCause() instanceof MismatchedInputException bad && !bad.getPath().isEmpty()) {
            String field = bad.getPathReference().replaceAll("^.*\\[\"|\"\\]$", "");
            String message = bad instanceof UnrecognizedPropertyException
                    ? "is not a known field"
                    : "has the wrong type";
            return ResponseEntity.unprocessableContent()
                    .body(unprocessable(UnprocessableException.invalidFields(List.of(new FieldViolation(field, message)))));
        }
        ProblemDetail problem = ProblemDetail.forStatusAndDetail(HttpStatus.BAD_REQUEST, "The body couldn't be parsed as JSON.");
        return ResponseEntity.badRequest().body(complete(problem));
    }

    @Override
    protected ResponseEntity<Object> handleNoResourceFoundException(NoResourceFoundException ex,
            HttpHeaders headers, HttpStatusCode status, WebRequest request) {
        ProblemDetail problem = ProblemDetail.forStatusAndDetail(HttpStatus.NOT_FOUND,
                "Nothing at " + ex.getHttpMethod() + " /" + ex.getResourcePath() + ".");
        return createResponseEntity(problem, headers, status, request);
    }

    @Override
    protected ResponseEntity<Object> createResponseEntity(Object body, HttpHeaders headers,
            HttpStatusCode status, WebRequest request) {
        if (body instanceof ProblemDetail problem) {
            complete(problem);
        }
        return super.createResponseEntity(body, headers, status, request);
    }
```

- `MethodArgumentNotValidException` is what `@Valid` throws when Bean Validation fails. Spring answers it with 400 by default, and Roombook turns it into the same `validation-failed` problem the service throws. A field error marked as a binding failure is a value that wouldn't convert.
- `HttpMessageNotReadableException` is what Spring throws when Jackson can't read the body. When Jackson's own exception underneath is a `MismatchedInputException` with a path, the JSON was readable, but a field had the wrong type or wasn't known, so it's a 422 on that field. Anything else is broken JSON, a 400.
- `handleNoResourceFoundException` replaces Spring's detail about a static resource with one that names the method and path.
- `createResponseEntity` is the base class's last step for every answer it builds, so overriding it puts `complete()` on all of them, the 405 and 415 included.

Spring Boot also has a setting, `spring.mvc.problemdetails.enabled=true`, that turns on a ready-made advice like this one. Roombook writes its own, since it needs its own types and fields.

## A safe 500

Anything the handler doesn't recognize is a bug or an outage. Log it in full, with the request id, and answer the generic `internal-error` problem. Never put the error's message in the response. `connection to the database was lost` tells a stranger which way to push, and a message from a library can include file paths, SQL, or worse.
